{"name":"Olive Grove","hex":"#716a4d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"olive grove","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cluster of olive trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/olive_grove"}],"entries":[{"title":"Olive Grove","blocks":[{"text":"Olive Grove was The Wednesday F.C.'s first permanent football ground, home to the club for just over a decade at the end of the 19th century. It was located on the site of what is now Sheffield City Council's Olive Grove Depot, near Queens Road in the centre of Sheffield. \nIn November 2009 it was announced that the world's oldest football club, Sheffield F.C., plan to return to its Sheffield roots with an 'iconic' 10,000 capacity stadium 1/4 of a mile away from the original Olive Grove Ground."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Leaflet_advert_for_blackburn_rovers_match-1887.jpg/330px-Leaflet_advert_for_blackburn_rovers_match-1887.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_Grove","wikidata":"Q7087158"},{"title":"Olive","blocks":[{"text":"The cultivated olive is a subspecies of subtropical evergreen tree in the family Oleaceae. Modern cultivars are traced primarily to the Near East, Aegean Sea, and Strait of Gibraltar. Olea europaea is the type species for its genus, Olea, which lends its name to the Oleaceae plant family. The olive fruit is classed botanically as a drupe, similar in structure and function to the cherry or peach. The term oil was originally synonymous with olive oil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Olivesfromjordan.jpg/330px-Olivesfromjordan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive","wikidata":"Q1621080"},{"title":"Grove (nature)","blocks":[{"text":"A grove is a small group of trees with minimal or no undergrowth, or a small orchard planted for the cultivation of fruits or nuts. Naturally occurring groves are typically small, perhaps a few acres at most, such as a sequoia grove. In contrast, orchards, which are normally intentional planting of trees, may be small or very large, like the apple orchards in Washington state, orange groves in Florida and olive groves in Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Ppalmeral.jpg/330px-Ppalmeral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grove_(nature)","wikidata":"Q1510380"},{"title":"Olive (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Olive, also called olive green, is a dark shade of yellowish green, like that of unripe or green olives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg/330px-Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_(color)","wikidata":"Q864152"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Olive_Grove_Stadium%2C_Sheffield.jpg/250px-Olive_Grove_Stadium%2C_Sheffield.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/Olive_Grove_Stadium%2C_Sheffield.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Olive_Grove_Stadium%2C_Sheffield.jpg/250px-Olive_Grove_Stadium%2C_Sheffield.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Olive Grove Stadium, Sheffield","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOlive_Grove_Stadium%2C_Sheffield.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg/500px-Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_